   "stem": {
      "text": "The functions \\( f \\), \\( g \\), and \\( h \\) are defined for all real numbers, and \\( h(x) \\neq 0 \\) for all \\( x \\neq 2 \\). It is known that \\[ \\lim_{x \\to 2} f(x) = 3, \\quad \\lim_{x \\to 2} g(x) = -1, \\quad \\lim_{x \\to 2} h(x) = 0, \\quad \\lim_{x \\to 2} \\frac{h(x)}{x - 2} = 4. \\]"
   },
   "skills": [
      "BC-SKL-01018",
      "BC-SKL-01019",
      "BC-SKL-01020",
      "BC-SKL-01022"
   ],
   "parts": [
      {
         "id": "a",
         "prompt": "Find each of the following limits: \\( \\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to 2} \\left[ 2f(x) - 5g(x) \\right] \\), \\( \\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to 2} f(x)\\left[ g(x) \\right]^{2} \\), and \\( \\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to 2} \\frac{f(x) + g(x)}{x + 1} \\).",
         "setup_required": false,
         "answer_latex": "11, \\quad 3, \\quad \\frac{2}{3}",
         "worked_solution": [
            {
               "text": "Sum and constant multiple theorems.",
               "latex": "\\lim_{x \\to 2} \\left[ 2f(x) - 5g(x) \\right] = 2(3) - 5(-1) = 11"
            },
            {
               "text": "Product theorem.",
               "latex": "\\lim_{x \\to 2} f(x)\\left[ g(x) \\right]^{2} = 3(-1)^{2} = 3"
            },
            {
               "text": "Quotient theorem; the denominator tends to 3, which is not 0.",
               "latex": "\\lim_{x \\to 2} \\frac{f(x) + g(x)}{x + 1} = \\frac{3 + (-1)}{2 + 1} = \\frac{2}{3}"
            }
         ],
         "points": [
            {
               "point_id": "a1",
               "point_type_id": "BC-PT-99004",
               "skills": [
                  "BC-SKL-01018",
